Evaluation
==========

Reproducible evaluation of the features on public datasets: one command,
pinned Zeek and Python images, fixed Zeek hash seeds, checksummed data. Two
runs give the same results.txt.

Data
----

Not versioned. Put them in eval/data/ (or pass DATA=...):

  Monday/Tuesday/Wednesday/Thursday/Friday-WorkingHours.pcap,
  GeneratedLabelledFlows.zip
      CICIDS-2017, https://www.unb.ca/cic/datasets/ids-2017.html
  ctu13/botnet-capture-*.pcap (neris, rbot, donbot, qvod)
      CTU-13 scenarios 42, 44, 47, 49,
      https://mcfp.felk.cvut.cz/publicDatasets/CTU-Malware-Capture-Botnet-<N>/

data.sha256 lists the expected files.

Run
---

  make eval            # check data, extract features (~10 min), evaluate
  make results         # evaluate again from out/

Results go to out/results.txt.

Protocol
--------

Fixed before measuring; nothing is tuned on the test data.

- Features: the package with default settings, Zeek 9.0.0, -C (CICIDS
  checksums are offloaded). Private ranges are local (Zeek default), so
  172.16.0.1, the CICIDS attacker behind NAT, is profiled.
- Labels: a (host, 5-min window) takes the attack of any labelled CICIDS
  flow that host originated in it. CSV times are local (UTC-3), day/month,
  12-hour without AM/PM. The internal scan of the Thursday infiltration is
  not labelled in the CSVs; it is added from the dataset documentation
  (192.168.10.8, 15:04-15:45 local).
- Detector: IsolationForest on log1p(features), trained on Monday (benign
  only). 10 seeds, mean (and range) reported.
- Metrics: AUC, and the share of attack windows detected at equal
  false-alarm rates (0.1, 0.5, 1 % of the test day's benign windows). These
  thresholds use the test labels: they compare feature sets, they are not
  deployable thresholds.
- Feature sets: Baseline = n_conns, bytes_out, bytes_in (what NetFlow
  counters give); HostProfile = all columns.
- Days: Thursday and Friday were used while developing the features.
  Tuesday was the first holdout: nothing was tuned on it, but it has been
  looked at (first with an alert threshold fixed on Monday, which compared
  feature sets at unequal false-alarm rates and was replaced by the metric
  above). It is no longer a clean holdout.
- CTU-13: windows of the infected host (147.32.84.165) vs all CICIDS benign
  windows, each rhythm feature alone; no setting tuned on these captures.

Planned holdout: Wednesday
--------------------------

Declared 2026-10-13, before Wednesday-WorkingHours.pcap was ever processed.
Its attacks (DoS Slowloris, Slowhttptest, Hulk, GoldenEye; Heartbleed) were
never seen while developing the features or the detector.

Chosen detector, fixed: the generic one above (global IsolationForest,
trained on Monday, no other setting). It looks for anything unusual, not
for known attacks. Wednesday is run once with evaluate.py as it is, with
the metrics above, and reported as is.

Run once on 2026-10-13, no code change (only its checksum added to
data.sha256); results reported as is in the top-level README.

Explored and not retained (compare.py, Thursday and Friday only): per-host
z-score and per-host quantile scores. They catch a known host that changes
behavior far better (Infiltration: 3 -> 53 % at 0.1 % false alarms; first
detections of the Ares bot) but miss hosts without history (attacks from
172.16.0.1) and are limited by one day of history (about 70 windows per
host). Per-host scoring needs a longer history: future work.

Limits
------

- Few attack windows (2 to 166 per attack): one window moves a percentage.
- CICIDS benign traffic is scripted, more regular than real traffic.
- Attacks from 172.16.0.1 are partly separated by frac_internal_dst because
  the attacker sits behind an internal NAT, a dataset artefact.
- CTU-13 and CICIDS are different networks and years.
